    "evidence_text": "To analyze pretrained language model performance on QA, we extend the LAMA (Petroni et al., 2019) evaluation framework by a component that is focused on negation.\n\nis reformulated as \u201cThe theory of relativity was developed by [MASK].\u201d.\n\nThis setup\n\nallows for unsupervised open domain question answering.\n\nThis work analyzes the understanding of pretrained language models of factual and commonsense knowledge stored in negated statements.\n\nTo this end, we introduce the negated LAMA dataset.\n\nWe find that the predicted filler words often have high overlap.\n\nWe identify and analyze a subset of cloze statements where predictions are different.\n\nThe underlying Open Mind Common Sense corpus provides matching statements to query the language model.\n\nSQuAD  is a standard question answering dataset.\n\nLAMA contains a subset of 305 context-insensitive questions and provides manually reformulated cloze-style statements to query the model.\n\nWe did the same for a subset of ConceptNet that is easy to negate.\n\nWe selected this subset by filtering for sentence length and extracting common queries.\n\nModels\tWe use the source code provided by  and  and evaluate using Transformer-XL large\n\nResults\tTable TABREF1 compares the predictions of original LAMA and negated LAMA.\n\nTable TABREF4 gives examples of BERT-large predictions.\n\nand \u201cBirds cannot fly.\u201d.\n\nA key problem is that the LAMA setup does not\n\nallow to refrain from giving an answer.\n\nGenerally, prediction probabilities drop in the negated statements, which would suggest the existence of a threshold to filter answers.\n\nNo common threshold can be found.\n\nWe give two examples of such patterns.\n\nPeople would refer\n\nConcerning negation the following papers are of interest:  analyze the grammatical knowledge captured by BERT.\n\nIn a case study, they test for correct licensing environments for negative polarity items.\n\nThey study a set of classifiers distinguishing between grammatically correct and incorrect sentences.\n\nWe take a different approach by focusing on factual knowledge stored in negated statements.\n\ninvestigate the understanding of function words \u2013 among them negation particles \u2013 using an entailment- and classification-based approach.\n\nThe models are fine-tuned to the task of interest.\n\nWe on the other hand question to what extend factual knowledge present in\n\nnegated statements is indirectly acquired during pretraining.\n\nNegation is examined using a dataset of 72 simple sentences querying for category membership.\n\nWe try to maximize the change in semantics and invert meaning.\n\nConclusion\tWe show that pretrained language models have problems handling negation.\n\nOutput predictions for the original LAMA query and the negated statement are highly correlated.\n\nmaking knowledge graphs currently a more reliable choice for question answering.",
